 "segments": [
  {
   "id": 0,
   "start": 0.9,
   "end": 5.7,
   "text": " Reactive rules work well when the programmer already knows every rule in advance."
  },
  {
   "id": 1,
   "start": 6.56,
   "end": 12.84,
   "text": " But what if an engine is placed in an unknown maze and has to learn the best strategy of it all?"
  },
  {
   "id": 2,
   "start": 14.08,
   "end": 16.4,
   "text": " That is reinforcement learning."
  },
  {
   "id": 3,
   "start": 16.98,
   "end": 21.24,
   "text": " Instead of giving the engine the rules, we give it a numeric reward."
  },
  {
   "id": 4,
   "start": 21.68,
   "end": 28.14,
   "text": " For example, plus 10 for reaching the charging goals, minus 10 for hitting a hazard piece,"
  },
  {
   "id": 5,
   "start": 28.14,
   "end": 35.48,
   "text": " and minus 1 for every step it takes so it learns to hurry instead of walking in circles."
  },
  {
   "id": 6,
   "start": 36.48,
   "end": 41.52,
   "text": " In query learning, the agents give a reference table called a query table."
  },
  {
   "id": 7,
   "start": 42.34,
   "end": 52.6,
   "text": " Each row is a state, a square on the grid, and each column is a possible action, up, down, left, or right."
  },
  {
   "id": 8,
   "start": 53.32,
   "end": 61.02,
   "text": " The number inside itself, called the query value, estimates the total future reward of taking the action from the square."
